+1.1 By [A1] and $\beta>0$, every configuration satisfies $0\le e^{-\beta U_N}\le e^{\beta BN}$, with the value $0$ when $U_N=+\infty$. Fact [F7] makes the integrand and its restriction to $\Lambda_L^N$ measurable. Let $s=\sum_{j=1}^r c_j\mathbf1_{A_j}$ be any nonnegative simple minorant of $e^{-\beta U_N}\mathbf1_{\Lambda_L^N}$, written with disjoint measurable sets and finite coefficients $c_j\ge0$ as in [L1]. Outside $\Lambda_L^N$ the minorant is zero, so every $A_j$ with $c_j>0$ is contained in $\Lambda_L^N$; on each such set $c_j\le e^{\beta BN}$. Since [F2] gives a finite measure for the box, each such $A_j$ has finite measure. Countable additivity gives finite additivity on these disjoint sets, while monotonicity follows by decomposing the box into their union and its measurable complement. Consequently $$\int s\,d\lambda_d^{\otimes N}=\sum_{c_j>0}c_j\lambda_d^{\otimes N}(A_j) \le e^{\beta BN}\sum_{c_j>0}\lambda_d^{\otimes N}(A_j) =e^{\beta BN}\lambda_d^{\otimes N}\!\left(\bigcup_{c_j>0}A_j\right) \le e^{\beta BN}\lambda_d^{\otimes N}(\Lambda_L^N).$$ Taking the supremum over these minorants in [L1] proves the same bound for the restricted integrand's nonnegative integral. [A1, F1, F7, L1, algebra]
 
